{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 6, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a prominent nobleman who ruled as the Count of Tripoli during the twelfth century. His life was defined by complex political struggles within the Crusader states and his relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He served as regent for the young King Baldwin IV and later for his cousin Baldwin V. Raymond faced constant threats from Saladin and internal rivals like Guy of Lusignan. His family lineage connected him to major European powers yet he spent his entire adult life in the Levant.\n\n*Early Life and Inheritance*\nRaymond was born in 1140 to Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His father died in 1152 when Raymond was only twelve years old. The count of Tripoli needed a regent because he was a minor. The Kingdom of Jerusalem intervened to ensure stability in the northern county. Raymond eventually came of age and took full control of his territories. He expanded his influence through strategic marriages and alliances. His mother Hodierna played a significant role in securing his position during his youth. He inherited vast lands and military obligations that shaped his early career as a leader.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nThe death of King Amalric in 1174 placed Raymond in a position of immense power. He became the guardian of the leper king Baldwin IV. This role made him the most powerful man in the realm for several years. Raymond tried to stabilize the kingdom while facing external threats from Muslim forces. He clashed with the Order of Templars and other factions within the court. His relationship with Baldwin IV soured over time due to differing political strategies. Raymond later supported the succession of Baldwin V but faced opposition from Guy of Lusignan. The internal divisions weakened the Crusader states before the Battle of Hattin.\n\n*Later Years and Death*\nRaymond died in 1187 near Tiberias just before the catastrophic Battle of Hattin. He had fallen out of favor with the king but remained a key figure in the north. His death removed a stabilizing force from the political landscape of the Levant. The county of Tripoli passed to his distant relative Raymond of Poitiers. The legacy of Raymond III remains controversial among historians due to his shifting alliances. Some view him as a pragmatic leader while others see him as a traitor to the cause. His life illustrates the fragility of the Crusader states during this turbulent era. The loss of his leadership contributed to the eventual fall of Jerusalem later that year.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0025742378}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.0728842e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0047333026}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.10912456}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0032537407}, {"token": " prominent", "logprob": -0.7315803}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.037290353}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.10891897}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -0.23106402}, {"token": " ruled", "logprob": -0.555903}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -2.7688117}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.0019292355}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.00023528721}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.0032531428}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.023248801}, {"token": " tw", "logprob": -0.013998519}, {"token": "elfth", "logprob": -1.1086526e-05}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -1.7881409e-06}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.109057955}, {"token": " His", "logprob": -0.5307346}, {"token": " life", "logprob": -0.017657345}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.1428781}, {"token": " defined", "logprob": -0.977153}, {"token": " by", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " complex", "logprob": -0.18646182}, {"token": " political", "logprob": -0.0001728088}, {"token": " struggles", "logprob": -1.013566}, {"token": " within", "logprob": -0